{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794393045,"nonce":"/VOzlrbE6KSExknyL7eebFkjcRE=","message":"Emscripten, same day. The user confirmed hxcpp through Emscripten is in scope, and `tests/counter/run.sh emcc` now passes under Node with hxcpp 4.3.2 and emcc 5.0.7 (emsdk at ~/Vibranium/emsdk): handles, text and bytes, thrown failures, futures with `await`, and `then`. hxn needed one change: `hxn.Future.then` polls from the event loop where there are no threads.\n\nGetting hxcpp itself to build and run took four workarounds, none of them about hxn:\n\n- hxcpp uses `CXX` from the environment in place of emcc. The script unsets it.\n- hxcpp assumes the host's 64-bit pointers. `-D HXCPP_M32`.\n- With UTF-16 strings on, hxcpp's Sys.cpp calls `_wsetlocale`, which only Windows has. `-D disable_unicode_strings`.\n- hxcpp links with JavaScript exceptions but does not compile with `-fexceptions`, so a Haxe `try` catches nothing. The binding's build XML adds the flag.\n\nStill broken or unverified there:\n\n- hxcpp's collector does not return from `cpp.vm.Gc.run(true)`; a plain program with no hxn code hangs the same way. So no finalizer has been seen to run, and the test skips that check.\n- hxcpp has no threads under Emscripten (`Thread.create` throws), so `await` on a future that is still pending can never return. Only `then` works for those.\n- A repeating event in hxcpp's event loop sometimes never fires. `then` therefore re-queues a one-shot event each turn, which spins while a future is outstanding.\n- Rust is built with `panic=abort` here too. The shipped std unwinds with wasm exceptions (`__cpp_exception`), which does not link against hxcpp's JavaScript-based ones.\n- The browser has not been tried; everything ran under Node.","files":null}]}